
%9Syntax:%9

@SYNTAX:fe_web@

%9Parameters:%9

    STATUS:     Displays whether the server is enabled, the number of
                connected clients and, for each client, its address, its
                server tag and the number of messages sent and received.

    Without any parameter, the status is displayed.

%9Description:%9

    Shows the status of fe-web, the WebSocket server that lets a web client
    (for example NexusIRC) show and use this erssi session from a browser.
    The fe_web module is loaded at startup by default (it is listed in the
    autoload_modules setting) when erssi is built with fe-web support.

    The server itself is controlled by the settings below. Changing any of
    them starts, stops or restarts the server, which disconnects the web
    clients; changing other settings does not touch it. When the server
    cannot start (the error says why), fix the cause and run /SET
    fe_web_enabled ON again: while it is not running, a /SET or /TOGGLE
    of any fe_web_ setting tries again, also with the value it has.

        fe_web_enabled (default: OFF)
            Runs the server.
        fe_web_port (default: 9001)
            The TCP port to listen on.
        fe_web_bind (default: 127.0.0.1)
            The address to listen on.
        fe_web_socket (default: empty)
            A Unix socket to listen on instead of fe_web_bind and
            fe_web_port; empty listens on TCP. "~/" stands for the home
            directory and a relative path is relative to the erssi
            directory (~/.erssi), so "fe-web.sock" is
            ~/.erssi/fe-web.sock.
        fe_web_password (default: empty)
            The password a client must give. The server refuses to start
            while it is empty. A client sends it in an "Authorization:
            Bearer" header of its upgrade request; when the request has no
            Authorization header at all, the "password" parameter of the
            URL (/?password=..., percent-encoded) is accepted too, as
            NexusIRC sends it. A URL can end up in the logs of proxies, so
            the header is the better way.

    Connections always use TLS (wss://) with a self-signed certificate.
    TLS is what protects them: have the web client pin that certificate,
    and do not let anything you do not trust end the TLS connection in
    between. The messages are also encrypted with AES-256-GCM, with a key
    derived from fe_web_password, but that layer protects nothing TLS
    does not: the client sends the password inside the same TLS
    connection, so whoever can read it (a man in the middle of a client
    that does not check the certificate, a TLS proxy or tunnel) has the
    key too; the key is the same for every session and both directions,
    and messages from a client are accepted without it as well.

    The certificate and its key are made
    the first time the server starts and kept in ~/.erssi/fe-web-cert.pem
    and ~/.erssi/fe-web-key.pem (mode 0600); a key that other users can
    read, or that is not yours, is replaced with a new one. A symbolic
    link, a directory or anything else that is not a regular file in
    place of either is neither used nor replaced: the server does not
    start and says which file it is; remove it, then /SET fe_web_enabled
    ON again (or restart). Nothing is written while fe_web_enabled is OFF.

    Keep fe_web_bind on 127.0.0.1 and put the web client behind a TLS proxy
    or tunnel if it has to be reached from elsewhere.

    On a shared shell box every local user can connect to a port on
    127.0.0.1. A Unix socket can be reached by you alone: the socket file
    is made with mode 0600 and, on Linux and the BSDs, connections from
    processes of other users are refused as well. TLS and the password
    work on it exactly as on TCP; the web client connects to the socket
    path and checks the certificate for the name localhost.

    After 5 wrong passwords from one peer, that peer gets one check every
    2 seconds. On the socket, and on Linux also on a loopback port, a peer
    is a user, and your own are never slowed down. On the BSDs and macOS
    erssi cannot tell who connects to a loopback port: every local user,
    you as well, is the one peer 127.0.0.1, so another user's wrong
    passwords slow down your own web client's logins. There fe_web_socket
    is the only setting that keeps the users of a shared box apart.

    The directory of the socket must be yours and writable by nobody else
    (a missing one is made with mode 0700), otherwise the server does not
    start. A socket left behind by an erssi that did not exit cleanly is
    replaced; any other file at the path is left alone and the server does
    not start. The socket is removed when the server stops.

    Networks and servers: a web client can list, add, change and remove
    them. Passwords (sasl_password, a server's password and tls_pass)
    and a network's autosendcmd, which often holds a NickServ password,
    are sent to it as "***"; a client that sends "***" back leaves the
    stored value as it is. Each change is written to the config file at
    once, as the autosave would write it: with settings_autosave ON, a
    config file read without errors and not changed outside erssi.
    Otherwise it is kept until /SAVE, and the client is told so.

    Web clients and E2E: with the e2e module loaded, a web client is told
    the E2E state of every channel and query (off, on, waiting for keys,
    blocked, keyring error) and the key requests that wait there, and it
    can accept or decline such a request. Accepting hands out your key, so
    only a request that waits in that channel or query, with exactly the
    ident@host the client names (and the key it showed, when it sends
    the fingerprint), is acted on; erssi prints what was done
    in that window. While the rpe2e.pl script is loaded, web clients see
    E2E as off and their actions are refused. The protocol (version 1) is
    docs/fe-web-e2e.md in the erssi sources.

%9Examples:%9

    /SET fe_web_password a-long-random-secret
    /SET fe_web_bind 127.0.0.1
    /SET fe_web_enabled ON
    /SET fe_web_socket ~/.erssi/fe-web.sock
    /SET -clear fe_web_socket
    /FE_WEB
    /FE_WEB STATUS

%9See also:%9 E2E, LOAD, SET

